-- SessionRuntimeEvent(type='text_delta', data={'content': '## 
Change\n\n**`src/components/ResponsiveNav.tsx`**\n- Extracted the destinations 
into a `NAV_LINKS` array: `Board` (`#board`), `Activity` (`#activity`), plus the
new `Reports` (`#reports`).\n- Added active-destination state seeded from 
`window.location.hash`, kept in sync with `hashchange` (so URL-driven navigation
also updates the active link).\n- Clicking a link sets it active and closes the 
mobile menu (`setOpen(false)`), which also drives the Menu button\'s 
`aria-expanded` back to `false`.\n- Only the active link receives 
`aria-current="page"` (others get `undefined`, so no attribute is 
rendered).\n\n**`src/styles.css`**\n- Added an `.nav-links 
a[aria-current="page"]` style (bold white + underline) so the active state is 
visibly distinguishable, not just semantic.\n\nDesktop/resize behavior needed no
new CSS: the base `.nav-links { display: flex }` applies above the breakpoint 
and the hiding rules live inside `@media (max-width: 760px)`, so a menu closed 
on mobile becomes visible again when resized to desktop. The `open` class adds 
no inline styling outside the media query, so it cannot keep links hidden on 
desktop.\n\n## Verification actually run\n\n```\nnpm run verify:agent\n  -> npm 
run build   (tsc --noEmit && vite build)  ✅\n  -> npm run test:public 
(playwright, 6 tests)     ✅ 6 passed\n```\n\nNo test files were modified or 
deleted.\n\n## Remaining risks\n\n- I verified the breakpoint behavior by 
reading the CSS cascade, not with a new automated desktop/resize test (adding 
tests was out of scope); `test:public` exercises only the existing mobile 
open/close case.\n- Initial load with no hash leaves no link marked 
`aria-current`, which is correct for "no active destination" but means a page 
loading at `#top` shows no active link.', 'aid': 0})
